CAT = "iepsaEmergency"; SEG = "EMG"
# QQI 4N20604 learning outcomes 1.8 (emergencies including terrorist and extremist actions; an injured
# person), 1.11 (alarms, evacuation), 1.14 (on-site safety and fire equipment); Safety, Health and
# Welfare at Work Act 2005 s. 13. Emergency numbers 112 and 999 (Ireland).
Q = [
("Which numbers call the emergency services in Ireland?",
 "112 or 999", ["911 only", "101 or 111", "000 only"],
 "In Ireland both 112 and 999 reach the emergency services (Garda, ambulance, fire service, coast guard), free from any phone."),
("The fire alarm sounds in an office block. What is a guard's main job?",
 "Help everyone get out", ["Find and fight the fire", "Collect the cash first", "Reset the alarm panel"],
 "A guard follows the site's fire plan: help people out by the nearest safe exit, check areas if it is safe, and meet the fire service. Fighting a fire is only for a small fire you are trained for."),
("Where do people go after leaving a building in a fire evacuation?",
 "The assembly point", ["The car park exit road", "Their own cars", "Back to their desks"],
 "People gather at the agreed assembly point so a roll call can show who is missing. Nobody goes back in until the fire service says it is safe."),
("Why is the visitor log taken to the assembly point in an evacuation?",
 "To account for visitors", ["To get visitors' feedback", "To collect parking fees", "To book the next meeting"],
 "The log shows which visitors are on site, so the roll call can account for them as well as staff."),
("Which fire extinguisher has a black label band and suits electrical fires?",
 "Carbon dioxide", ["Water", "Foam", "Wet chemical"],
 "CO2 extinguishers, marked with a black band, suit electrical equipment and flammable liquids. Water and foam conduct electricity and must not be used on live electrics."),
("Which extinguisher is designed for a deep-fat fryer fire in a kitchen?",
 "Wet chemical", ["Water", "Carbon dioxide", "Dry powder"],
 "Wet chemical extinguishers (yellow band) are made for cooking oil and fat fires (class F). Water on burning oil causes a violent flare-up."),
('A fire involving spilled petrol in a car park is which class?',
 'Class B', ['Class A', 'Class C', 'Class F'],
 'Class B fires involve flammable liquids such as petrol, diesel and paint. Class A is solids like wood and paper, class C gases and class F cooking oils.'),
("What is the first thing to do before using a fire extinguisher on a small fire?",
 "Make sure the alarm is raised", ["Open all the windows", "Phone your supervisor", "Read the full manual on the extinguisher"],
 "Always raise the alarm first and keep a clear exit behind you. Only then tackle a small fire, and only if trained and it is safe."),
("Which word sums up the use of an extinguisher: pull, aim, squeeze and...?",
 "Sweep", ["Spray up", "Shake", "Stop"],
 "Pull the pin, aim at the base of the fire, squeeze the handle and sweep from side to side."),
('A bomb threat arrives by email instead of by phone. What should the guard do?',
 'Keep it and report it', ['Delete it straight away', 'Reply to the sender', 'Forward it to all staff'],
 "The message is evidence. Do not reply to, forward or delete it; report it at once under the site's bomb threat procedure and let the Gardaí and the client decide on next steps."),
("A guard finds an unattended bag with wires showing in a lobby. What should he do?",
 "Clear the area and report", ["Open it to check it", "Carry it outside", "Kick it to test it"],
 "Do not touch or move it. Clear the area, keep people back, report it from a safe distance and follow the site's suspicious item procedure."),
('After clearing people away from a suspicious item, what should the guard do next?',
 'Stop anyone going back', ['Go back to photograph it', 'Cover it with a coat', 'Pour water over it'],
 'Hold the cordon so nobody returns, report from a safe distance and wait for the Gardaí. Covering, wetting or moving the item could set it off.'),
("Which behaviour could suggest hostile reconnaissance of a site?",
 "Filming entrances and cameras", ["Asking for directions once", "Buying a coffee", "Waiting for a bus"],
 "Someone repeatedly filming or noting entrances, cameras and guard routines may be planning an attack or crime. Report it with a good description."),
("If an armed attack happens in a public building, what is the general advice for people to do first?",
 "Escape if it is safe", ["Confront the attacker", "Film it on a phone", "Stay to help clear up"],
 "Published public-safety advice, such as the UK's Run, Hide, Tell, is to escape if you can do so safely, hide if you cannot, and tell the emergency services when it is safe. Guards follow the site's lockdown plan."),
("You find a person collapsed and not moving. What do you check first?",
 "Danger to yourself", ["Their wallet for ID", "Their phone for contacts", "The CCTV footage"],
 "First check the scene is safe for you, then check the person's response and breathing and call for help. You cannot help anyone if you become a casualty too."),
("A collapsed man is not breathing normally. What should a guard do after calling 112 or 999?",
 "Start CPR, send for an AED", ["Give him some water", "Sit him upright", "Wait for him to wake"],
 "A person who is unresponsive and not breathing normally needs CPR straight away, with an AED (defibrillator) fetched as soon as possible."),
("A visitor is bleeding heavily from a cut arm. What is the first aid priority?",
 "Firm direct pressure", ["Wash it under a tap", "Apply a hot towel", "Raise the legs only"],
 "Firm direct pressure on the wound, with a dressing if available, controls most bleeding. Call for help if bleeding is severe."),
("A guard is not trained in first aid and a person is hurt. What should he do?",
 "Get help, stay with them", ["Leave and finish patrol", "Move them to a car", "Give them painkillers"],
 "Summon the site's first aider or emergency services, keep the person comfortable and reassured, and record the incident."),
('What should a guard tell the fire service when they arrive?',
 'Fire location, anyone missing', ["The site's opening hours", "The guards' shift rota", "The client's phone number"],
 'The fire service needs to know where the fire is, who may still be inside (from the roll call and visitor log) and any hazards such as gas or chemicals.'),
("A flood starts from a burst pipe at night. What should the guard do?",
 "Shut off the water if safe", ["Wait for staff to arrive", "Move the alarm panel", "Mop it up alone for the rest of the night"],
 "Use the stopcock if trained and safe, keep clear of electrics in water, call the keyholder or maintenance and record the incident. Guarding includes preventing loss by flood."),
("Why must escape routes and fire exits be kept clear at all times?",
 "So people can get out", ["To help deliveries", "To keep floors clean", "To save on heating"],
 "Blocked routes can trap people in a fire. Guards report and clear obstructions on patrol."),
('When the fire service arrives at a site, where should the guard meet them?',
 'At the agreed access point', ['Inside the burning room', 'At his own car', 'On the roof of the building'],
 'The fire plan names a point where the guard meets the fire service to hand over the keys, the alarm panel location and the roll call. Never go back inside a burning building.'),
("During an evacuation, a person who uses a wheelchair is on the second floor. What should happen?",
 "Follow their evacuation plan", ["Leave them where they are", "Carry them down alone", "Ask them to wait for the lift"],
 "Sites prepare personal evacuation plans for people who need help, often a refuge point and trained assistance. Follow it and tell the fire service where they are."),
("Under the 2005 Act, why must a guard attend fire drills and safety training his employer requires?",
 "It is an employee duty", ["Only if paid extra", "It is entirely optional", "Only if he agrees to"],
 "Section 13(1)(f) requires an employee to attend training and undergo assessment that the employer reasonably requires for safety, health and welfare at work."),
]
